@namespace AuthWithAdmin.Client.Pages.Student
@using AuthWithAdmin.Shared.AuthSharedModels
@using AuthWithAdmin.Client.Services
@using AuthWithAdmin.Client.Components
@inject IStudentProfileService ProfileService

@*
    StudentProfilePage — "הפרופיל שלי"
    Source of truth: design-reference/motiva/project/Motiva Profile.dc.html,
    LIGHT presentation only. (The same file's dark shell is a palette
    reference, not a second screen — Motiva - System Master.dc.html:453-459.)

    Not routable: SettingsPage.razor owns /settings and renders this for
    students only. Mentor / Lecturer / Admin keep StaffSettingsPage.

    STRUCTURE — the page reads Identity -> Personal -> Team -> Preferences,
    and is built so it cannot read any other way:

      StudentProfileIdentityHero   ambient band — avatar, name, role · team.
                                   Identity only; no fields, no controls.
      StudentProfilePersonalCard   "פרטים אישיים" — email (read-only),
                                   phone (editable), academic year. PRIMARY.
      StudentProfileTeamCard       "הצוות שלי" — team · project, the student
                                   roster and the mentors, from the project
                                   context the shell already caches.
      StudentProfileNotificationsCard
                                   "התראות במייל" — the real per-type email
                                   switches. LAST, and the only card on a
                                   plain surface rather than AmbientEdge, so
                                   preferences sit visually below the two
                                   content cards instead of leading the page.

    Personal and Team pair on the first row above 1360px; preferences span
    the full width beneath them. Everything stacks below 1360px, in the same
    order.

    The Master draws email and phone inside the hero; they are in a card here
    because a hero that carries an inline editor reads as a form, not as an
    introduction.

    The page is deliberately shorter than the design file:

      * Email is rendered read-only with NO edit affordance. The Master
        draws an inline editor for it, but no endpoint mutates users.Email
        and it is the login identity, so an editable-looking field would
        promise something the system cannot do.

      * The "מראה ותצוגה" (light/dark/system) card is not rendered. Dark
        mode has not been audited across the already-migrated student
        screens, so shipping the selector would imply a supported feature.
        Nothing was deleted: ThemePreference, PUT /api/student/me/preferences
        and gradify.setTheme are all untouched and still drive the theme.

      * Two of the Master's notification rows have no NotificationType
        behind them and are therefore absent rather than disabled — see
        StudentProfileNotificationsCard.

    Also intentionally absent, all with their backends fully intact: the
    Slack / Google Calendar integrations card, ID number (empty for every
    seeded user), ProjectContextDto's TrackName (an untranslated English
    value — see StudentProfileTeamCard), the milestone / task / progress half
    of ProjectContextDto (that is Project Details, not Profile), and the
    legacy UserPreferences notification toggles (which duplicate
    UserNotificationPreferences).
*@

@if (_loading)
{
    <div class="spf-page-state">
        <span class="spf-page-spinner" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span>טוענת פרטים…</span>
    </div>
}
else if (_profile is null)
{
    <div class="spf-page-state">
        <MEmptyState Live="true"
                     Title="אירעה שגיאה בטעינת הפרופיל"
                     Description="נסי לרענן את הדף." />
    </div>
}
else
{
    <StudentProfileIdentityHero Profile="_profile"
                                OnProfileChanged="StateHasChanged" />

    @* Master: "Profile caps its settings form at 920px — intentional, since
       a full-bleed form at desktop width is unreadable." That cap was written
       for the Master's two stacked cards; one narrow column left the real
       screen floating in an empty canvas. The cap is raised, not removed —
       see .spf-grid — and the readability the Master was protecting is now
       held by the two-column composition and the notification card's own row
       columns instead of by a narrow page column.

       Each card is wrapped in a cell this page owns. Without the wrappers the
       grid-placement rules below would have to select the cards themselves —
       and a card's root is rendered by MCard under ITS scope id, which a rule
       compiled into this page's scope can never match. *@
    <div class="spf-content">
        <div class="spf-grid">
            <div class="spf-cell">
                <StudentProfilePersonalCard Profile="_profile"
                                            OnProfileChanged="StateHasChanged" />
            </div>
            <div class="spf-cell">
                <StudentProfileTeamCard Profile="_profile" />
            </div>
            <div class="spf-cell spf-cell-prefs">
                <StudentProfileNotificationsCard />
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
}

@code {
    private StudentProfileDto? _profile;
    private bool _loading = true;

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        _profile = await ProfileService.GetProfileAsync();
        _loading = false;
    }
}
